From d5f3a5ff0fea2c438c769d5ce98acdb856ff7762 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 07:46:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: budget refusal names the file and
 the cap it hit

A gateway terminal ran a 950 KB, 17,340-line runner (under 1 MiB, over the
16384-line walk cap) and was refused with only "the scan budget was
exhausted (text at depth 1)" plus "must be a regular text file under
1 MiB", so the agent could not tell which file or limit to fix.

charge_text now records which limit refused the text (bytes, lines or the
longest line, with the cap or what the walk had left of it). The walk
threads the quoted source path into _budget_exhausted for referenced
scripts and their sh -c payloads; cron names its script (and prompt). So
the refusal reads e.g. "`/x/hermes-sync` has 17002 lines, over the
16384-line scan cap (text at depth 1)". Both caller messages now state the
real budget: "within the scan budget (1 MiB, 16384 lines, 64 KiB per
line)". Verdicts are unchanged (message only); paths/remote-read refusals
keep their wording.

Proof: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_budget_refusal.py is RED on
origin/main (8 of 9 original cases fail; the inert-mention control
passes) and GREEN here; 15 files importing lifecycle_guard /
terminal_tool_guards pass (871). 12/12 mutants of the added conditions
killed.
